WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.454
TraceTutor turns a programming error into a learning exercise. This edited walkthrough uses

00:00:04.454 --> 00:00:08.908
real browser captures and synthetic narration. The examples are fabricated. No pasted code is

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:13.362
uploaded or executed.

00:00:14.362 --> 00:00:19.161
Here a full Python traceback ends in division by zero. The parser focuses the final exception

00:00:19.161 --> 00:00:23.959
and reports student app, line eighteen. A small Naive Bayes model suggests the error family,

00:00:23.959 --> 00:00:28.758
then curated steps explain what to investigate.

00:00:29.758 --> 00:00:34.732
The learner answers a short question. Handling an undefined average explicitly receives correct

00:00:34.732 --> 00:00:39.706
feedback. Replacing the denominator with one would change the meaning. The downloadable

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:44.680
worksheet contains the lesson, without pasted text or private source paths.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:50.812
JavaScript stack traces work too. This example focuses an undefined property access at app dot

00:00:50.812 --> 00:00:55.944
J S, line forty two. The model uses forty synthetic examples across ten families. Its scores

00:00:55.944 --> 00:01:01.076
are not calibrated probabilities of correctness.

00:01:02.076 --> 00:01:06.651
An unsupported connection error gets an explicit not sure, instead of a guessed diagnosis.

00:01:06.651 --> 00:01:11.226
Twenty one narrow tests and real browser checks cover the prototype. Codex assisted with code,

00:01:11.226 --> 00:01:15.801
examples, tests and documentation. Team members should review and understand the implementation

00:01:15.801 --> 00:01:20.376
before presenting it.
